---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ic from '../../data/frames-ic.json';

const root = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const notebook = 'https://docs.minorplanetcenter.net/tutorials/notebooks/mpc_tutorial_orbit_frames/';
const epochDate = new Date(Date.UTC(1858, 10, 17) + ic.epochMJD_TT * 86400000).toISOString().slice(0, 10);

// Copy for each frame. Wording follows the MPC tutorial, which is authoritative.
const frames = [
  {
    id: 'barycentric', n: 1, name: 'Inertial barycentric', origin: 'Solar System barycentre', axes: 'Fixed',
    how: 'Nothing to transform: this is the frame the integration runs in, and the only one stored.',
    reveals: 'Newton’s laws hold with no fictitious forces. It also shows that the Sun moves: the inset follows the Sun’s own path, drawn to scale in solar radii, as Jupiter and Saturn pull it around the barycentre.',
    hides: 'Kamoʻoalewa’s relationship with Earth. Its orbit is just another ellipse.'
  },
  {
    id: 'heliocentric', n: 2, name: 'Inertial heliocentric', origin: 'Sun', axes: 'Fixed',
    how: 'Subtract the Sun’s position from everything.',
    reveals: 'Near-perfect Kepler ellipses. This is the frame of the MPC’s Cartesian and orbital elements.',
    hides: 'Everything interesting about this object: two unremarkable ellipses, and no hint of a resonance.'
  },
  {
    id: 'planetocentric', n: 3, name: 'Earth-centred, non-rotating', origin: 'Earth', axes: 'Fixed to the distant stars',
    how: 'Subtract Earth’s position instead of the Sun’s.',
    reveals: 'The honest test of “does it orbit Earth?” Kamoʻoalewa stays 0.1–0.35 AU away, ten to thirty times the radius of Earth’s Hill sphere (dashed circle), and its annual loops never enclose Earth.',
    hides: 'Why it stays close. The resonance that holds it there is only visible when the axes turn with Earth.'
  },
  {
    id: 'corotating', n: 4, name: 'Co-rotating with Earth', origin: 'Sun', axes: 'Rotate with Earth’s true longitude',
    how: 'Rotate every heliocentric position by −θ(t), Earth’s longitude, which pins Earth to the +x axis.',
    reveals: 'The famous quasi-satellite loops: once a year, retrograde, around Earth. The 1:1 resonance stands still and shows itself.',
    hides: 'Earth’s own eccentricity, which is silently absorbed into the frame’s uneven rotation.'
  },
  {
    id: 'synodic', n: 5, name: 'Uniform synodic', origin: 'Sun', axes: 'Rotate at Earth’s constant mean motion',
    how: 'Rotate by −(λ₀ + n t), a straight-line fit through Earth’s unwrapped longitude.',
    reveals: 'The frame of restricted three-body theory. Earth itself is no longer fixed: it traces its own small 2:1 eccentricity epicycle.',
    hides: 'Tidiness. With Earth no longer pinned in place, librations look less clean than in frame 4; this frame is where the theory lives, not the prettiest pictures.'
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title="Same orbit. Different point of view." description="One trajectory of the quasi-satellite Kamoʻoalewa, computed in your browser and seen from five reference frames.">
  <section class="inner-hero rf-hero">
    <div class="content-width inner-hero-content">
      <p class="eyebrow">INTERACTIVE TUTORIAL · REFERENCE FRAMES</p>
      <h1>Same orbit. Different point of view.</h1>
      <p>A reference frame is a choice of origin and of how the axes behave. Neither choice changes the physics, but both change what a trajectory looks like. Below, one integration of the quasi-satellite (469219) Kamoʻoalewa is computed in your browser, then drawn in five frames.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="site-content rf-lab" id="rf-lab">
    <div class="content-width rf-layout">
      <div class="rf-figure">
        <div class="rf-stage">
          <canvas id="rf-canvas" role="img" aria-label="Trajectory of Kamoʻoalewa in the selected reference frame"></canvas>
          <div class="rf-status" id="rf-status" role="status">Integrating 300 years of Solar System motion in your browser…</div>
        </div>
        <div class="rf-controls">
          <button id="rf-play" class="rf-button rf-play" type="button" aria-pressed="false" disabled>▶ Play</button>
          <label class="rf-scrub">
            <span class="visually-hidden">Time</span>
            <input id="rf-time" type="range" min="0" max="1" value="0" step="1" disabled />
          </label>
          <output id="rf-date" class="rf-date">{epochDate}</output>
        </div>
        <div class="rf-options">
          <label>Trail
            <select id="rf-trail">
              <option value="1">1 year</option>
              <option value="10" selected>10 years</option>
              <option value="50">50 years</option>
              <option value="10000">Everything so far</option>
            </select>
          </label>
          <label>Speed
            <select id="rf-speed">
              <option value="0.25">3 months / s</option>
              <option value="1" selected>1 year / s</option>
              <option value="5">5 years / s</option>
              <option value="20">20 years / s</option>
            </select>
          </label>
        </div>
        <dl class="rf-readout">
          <div><dt>Earth–Kamoʻoalewa distance</dt><dd id="rf-dist">–</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Longitude ahead of Earth</dt><dd id="rf-dlam">–</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Turns around Earth during the trail</dt><dd id="rf-wind">–</dd></div>
        </dl>
      </div>

      <div class="rf-side">
        <fieldset class="rf-frames">
          <legend>Reference frame</legend>
          {frames.map((f) => (
            <label class="rf-frame-option">
              <input type="radio" name="rf-frame" value={f.id} checked={f.id === 'corotating'} />
              <span class="rf-frame-n">{f.n}</span>
              <span class="rf-frame-text"><strong>{f.name}</strong><small>Origin: {f.origin} · Axes: {f.axes}</small></span>
            </label>
          ))}
        </fieldset>
        {frames.map((f) => (
          <div class="rf-explain" data-frame={f.id} hidden={f.id !== 'corotating'}>
            <h2>{f.n}. {f.name}</h2>
            <p><b>How.</b> {f.how}</p>
            <p><b>What it reveals.</b> {f.reveals}</p>
            <p><b>What it hides.</b> {f.hides}</p>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="site-content rf-prose">
    <div class="content-width rf-prose-inner">
      <h2>Wait: does Kamoʻoalewa orbit Earth or not?</h2>
      <p>Frame 3 shows loops that never enclose Earth. Frame 4 shows the same trajectory looping around Earth once a year. Neither plot is lying: both are drawn from the same stored numbers.</p>
      <p>Watch the <em>turns around Earth</em> readout with a 10-year trail. In the <button type="button" class="rf-inline" data-show-frame="corotating">co-rotating frame</button> the line from Earth to Kamoʻoalewa turns once a year, clockwise: −1 per year. But that frame itself rotates once a year, anticlockwise, with Earth: +1 per year. Undo the rotation, as the <button type="button" class="rf-inline" data-show-frame="planetocentric">non-rotating frame</button> does, and the two simply add to (−1) + (+1) = 0.</p>
      <p>The frame-independent fact is that Kamoʻoalewa is not bound to Earth: it orbits the Sun, well outside Earth’s Hill sphere. The loop is its eccentricity epicycle, about 0.1 AU by 0.2 AU for its eccentricity of 0.10. What makes it a quasi-satellite is where that loop sits: the 1:1 resonance keeps its mean longitude librating about Earth’s, so the loop is centred on Earth and encloses it.</p>
      <p>Choose the frame that makes your question easy, and know what it hides.</p>

      <h2>How this page works</h2>
      <p>When the page opens, your browser integrates the Sun, the eight planets, the Moon and Kamoʻoalewa for 300 years, starting on {epochDate}. Following the tutorial’s rule, the motion is integrated once, in the inertial barycentric frame, and stored. Every frame is then derived by subtracting an origin and rotating the axes; nothing is re-integrated when you switch.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><b>Initial conditions.</b> Kamoʻoalewa’s state vector is from the <a href="https://data.minorplanetcenter.net/api/get-orb" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">MPC Orbits API</a> (orbit fitted {ic.particles[0].mpc.fitted.slice(0, 10)} to {ic.particles[0].mpc.nobs} observations, {ic.particles[0].mpc.arc}). The Sun, planets and Moon are from <a href="https://ssd.jpl.nasa.gov/horizons/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">JPL Horizons</a>, with masses from DE440.</li>
        <li><b>Integrator.</b> A sixth-order symplectic method (Yoshida 1990) with a fixed half-day step, Newtonian point masses only. Over the full 300 years its Earth–Kamoʻoalewa separation agrees with REBOUND’s high-accuracy IAS15 integrator to within 30 km.</li>
        <li><b>Limitations.</b> Positions are projected onto the ecliptic plane. Relativity, asteroid perturbers and non-gravitational forces are omitted, and orbit uncertainty is not propagated, so this is a demonstration, not an ephemeris.</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="rf-links">
        <a class="text-link" href={notebook} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Read the full notebook ↗</a>
        <a class="text-link" href={`${root}teaching/`}>All tutorials →</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <script>
    import ic from '../../data/frames-ic.json';
    import { startReferenceFrames } from '../../scripts/frames/app.js';
    startReferenceFrames(ic);
  </script>
</BaseLayout>
